"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,458\nThe Mozart effect\n\n4\n00:00:12,258 --> 00:00:14,073\nPlay Mozart, get smarter.\n\n5\n00:00:14,413 --> 00:00:15,979\nIt became a news story,\n\n6\n00:00:16,049 --> 00:00:19,086\na parenting trend and a shelf of baby products.\n\n7\n00:00:19,438 --> 00:00:21,478\nIt started with one short paper.\n\n8\n00:00:21,648 --> 00:00:32,695\nIn 1993, Frances Rauscher and colleagues reported in Nature that college students did better on a spatial reasoning task after ten minutes of a Mozart sonata.\n\n9\n00:00:33,242 --> 00:00:34,713\nThe boost was small,\n\n10\n00:00:34,953 --> 00:00:37,427\nand it faded within about fifteen minutes.\n\n11\n00:00:37,868 --> 00:00:42,129\nRauscher stressed that the test measured only one kind of spatial ability.\n\n12\n00:00:42,299 --> 00:00:47,871\nBut headlines turned a brief effect on one kind of task into: Mozart makes you smarter.\n\n13\n00:00:48,261 --> 00:00:48,863\nAnd then: Mozart makes babies smarter,\n\n14\n00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:52,847\nwhich the study never tested.\n\n15\n00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,604\nBy the late 1990s,\n\n16\n00:00:55,730 --> 00:01:00,198\nmore news stories about the effect mentioned babies than college students.\n\n17\n00:01:00,368 --> 00:01:03,026\nOther labs found the result hard to replicate.\n\n18\n00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:10,784\nIn 2010, Jakob Pietschnig and colleagues pooled about forty studies with more than three thousand people.\n\n19\n00:01:11,226 --> 00:01:14,157\nCompared with silence, the effect was small.\n\n20\n00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:15,959\nCompared with other music,\n\n21\n00:01:16,063 --> 00:01:17,481\nit was nearly zero.\n\n22\n00:01:17,845 --> 00:01:21,997\nAnd there were signs that positive studies were more likely to be published.\n\n23\n00:01:22,167 --> 00:01:28,387\nThe grain of truth: a short burst of music you enjoy can lift mood and alertness for a while,\n\n24\n00:01:28,553 --> 00:01:29,887\nwhich may help briefly.\n\n25\n00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:31,811\nBut that is not Mozart,\n\n26\n00:01:31,831 --> 00:01:33,683\nand it is not intelligence.\n\n27\n00:01:33,853 --> 00:01:35,860\nThe bigger lesson is how a small,\n\n28\n00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,668\nshort lived finding became a lasting belief,\n\n29\n00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:40,735\nsimply by being repeated.\n\n30\n00:01:40,905 --> 00:01:41,998\nDo this instead.\n\n31\n00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:45,527\nTreat music in class or study as a mood choice,\n\n32\n00:01:45,547 --> 00:01:47,244\nnot a cognitive enhancer.\n\n33\n00:01:47,638 --> 00:01:49,464\nFor students who study with lyrics,\n\n34\n00:01:49,578 --> 00:01:53,768\nsuggest trying silence or instrumental music for hard reading.\n\n35\n00:01:54,332 --> 00:01:56,178\nOne tip to share with students: play music you enjoy for mood,\n\n36\n00:01:56,449 --> 00:01:59,649\nnot for IQ.\n\n37\n00:01:59,819 --> 00:02:01,753\nEnjoy Mozart for Mozart.\n\n38\n00:02:02,017 --> 00:02:03,381\nThat is reason enough.\n\n"